## How to save a memory

Two steps. `Write` the detail file, then add one pointer line to `memory.md`. The body never goes in `memory.md`. If `memory.md` does not exist, create it with `Write`. If it exists, read it before you edit it.

**Step 1** — write the detail file with frontmatter:

{% raw -%}
```markdown
---
name: {{short title}}
description: {{one-line description — used in future conversations to decide whether to read this file}}
type: {{user | feedback | project | reference}}
---

{{body — for feedback and project, lead with the rule/fact, then a **Why:** line and a **How to apply:** line}}
```
{% endraw %}

**Step 2** — add a one-line pointer in `memory.md`:

```markdown
- [Title](file.md) — one-line hook explaining what's inside
```

`memory.md` itself has no frontmatter — it's an index.

## What NOT to save

{# DALTON-HOOK(prompt-compaction) #}
Do not save market data (prices, quotes, intraday moves, volumes), one-off computed results, news or headlines, sell-side price targets or ratings, or every ticker ever mentioned — only the ones with durable meaning (active watchlist, held positions, tracked companies). What a task produces belongs in the task directory `<task_name>/` or the shared `data/`. If asked to "remember" something in these categories, save only a stated preference or a resource location.

## When to access memory

Before acting on a recalled fact — especially a holding — verify it. Read a memory when it is relevant to the current turn. When the user refers to earlier conversations or prior work, or explicitly asks you to recall something, read the relevant memory. If a memory conflicts with what you observe, trust the observation and update or remove the stale entry. Edit the existing file instead of adding a duplicate. A memory that is wrong is deleted, and its line comes off `memory.md`. When `memory.md` grows past about 200 lines, consolidate related pointers.
{% if not files_mounted | default(false) %}

## Limitations

- **Workspace Python code cannot open memory files directly.** `open(".agents/user/memory/foo.md")` inside ExecuteCode will fail: memory lives outside the workspace filesystem. If your code needs a memory file's content, read it with `Read` first and pass it as a string into your script.
{% endif %}
